        "question": {
          ":question/id": "16f1c90e-0ab9-5591-b853-44429096071c",
          ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
          ":question/problem": "Which of the following diagrams gives the slope field at the points indicated for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1x+(3)y}{2}?$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(3,1)$,$(-3,-1)$,$(-1,2)$.\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(3,1)}=\\frac{1(3)+(3)(1)}{2}=3, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-3,-1)}=\\frac{1(-3)+(3)(-1)}{2}=-3, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1,2)}=\\frac{1(-1)+(3)(2)}{2}=\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/repairs/2026-10-20-early-cohort/jobs/17592186172038/review-4-policy-2-64bbd1f805696f06/relocation-a-assets/789879e762e496af011356d70e4e0cb840aebb39966afb5b323d5695ccce8874.png)",
